#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125349} :activity/id #uuid "bfe361b1-984c-519f-8a6a-0ea85c15f6a8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048263 :topic/id #uuid "8cc35c33-61af-5ac3-971b-a33b8bbdcb9d" :topic/math-academy-id 1908} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125349 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125766 :tutorial/content "The set of vectors $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\}$ in an abstract vector space $V$ is **linearly dependent** if there exist real scalars $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{v}_1+x_2\\mathbf{v}_2+\\ldots+x_n\\mathbf{v}_n=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if $x_1 = x_2 = \\cdots = x_n = 0$ is the only solution, the set is **linearly independent**.\n\nLet's consider some examples.\n\n- First, let's consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ of all polynomials in variable $t,$ along with the set\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{ \\underbrace{1}_{\\color{blue}p_1(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{t}_{\\color{blue}p_2(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{t^2}_{\\color{blue}p_3(t)} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $S$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ we must solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for $p_1(t),p_2(t)$ and $p_3(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\cdot 1+{x}_{2}\\cdot t+{x}_{3}\\cdot {t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}t+{x}_{3}{t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0$ is the only solution, $S$ is linearly independent in $\\mathbb R[t].$\n\n- Let's consider the vector space $\\mathbb R[t]$ once more, along with the set \n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ \\underbrace{t^2+1}_{\\color{red}q_1(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{-2t^2-2}_{\\color{red}q_2(t)} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $T$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ we must solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{q}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}(t) & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}({t}^{2}+1)+{x}_{2}(-2{t}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ ({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})+({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has the nonzero solution $(x_1,x_2)=(2,1).$ As a result, we get\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot {\\color{red}q_1(t)} + 1 \\cdot {\\color{red}q_2(t)} = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\big\\{ q_1(t), q_2(t) \\big\\}$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb R[t].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "939069a1-d4fd-51b0-8c49-c1cdde533481" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3197 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "944f2e32-83df-59c4-9e62-dddd2a35c6d5" :step/math-academy-id 653140 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125353}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125350 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124491 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124304 :question/id #uuid "a788a6e6-8fb4-5657-912d-2eb8295a297f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4800"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407810}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"} :step/id #uuid "216d2c5a-6567-5c0a-8f69-8218d3f867f6" :step/math-academy-id 653148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125351 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124492 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124305 :question/id #uuid "7f0b6987-0ca3-5610-ab7b-6630c0f70191" :question/math-academy-id "e-4801"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdde35e9-7101-55f2-8452-ec1ef2d17d54" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050985}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407808}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficients That Show Linear Dependence Among Matrix Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "da0d1383-fa40-55ef-9635-eb9be7d2ca55" :step/math-academy-id 653144 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125352}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125352 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125767 :tutorial/content "Note the following important property of linearly dependent sets.\n\n*A set of vectors is linearly dependent if and only if it contains a vector that is a linear combination of **other** vectors from that set.*\n\nTo see why this is true, let $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2,\\cdots, \\mathbf{v}_n\\}$ be a linearly dependent set of $n$ vectors. This means that the equation\n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\cdots + x_n\\mathbf{v}_n = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a non-trivial solution, i.e. a solution in which at least one of the coefficients $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is not zero. Without loss of generality, we may assume that $x_1 \\ne 0,$ and we can rewrite the above equation as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 = -\\dfrac{x_2}{x_1} \\mathbf{v}_2 - \\dfrac{x_3}{x_1}\\mathbf{v}_3 - \\cdots - \\dfrac{x_n}{x_1}\\mathbf{v}_n.\n$$\n\nThe above argument shows that the vector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ can be written as a linear combination of the other vectors from the set." :tutorial/id #uuid "7600b92d-52bf-5c9a-bd48-5e9d12eb0d25" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3198 :tutorial/title "An Important Property of Linearly Dependent Sets"} :step/id #uuid "307549b1-c36a-5e02-9555-3fe8462e3c33" :step/math-academy-id 653147 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125350}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125353 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124493 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124306 :question/id #uuid "b8c2902e-9062-5417-9d19-a6a9dba986a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-4803"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99ce8f12-ee79-5384-8eb9-e9bfbb2534c5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048530}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407811}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficients That Show Linear Dependence Among Polynomial Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "e2b92597-3696-53e3-94fd-361b7b47d46c" :step/math-academy-id 653141 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125351}}] :activity/title "Linear Independence in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124714}]}
